.TH CHARON 1 "2026" "charon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
charon \- rclone remote-storage mount + bidirectional sync
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B charon
ferries files between any rclone remote and the local machine: it mounts the
remote via FUSE and keeps a fast local cache in step with it using Unison. It is
provider-neutral \(em Google Drive, Dropbox, or any rclone backend \(em and the
value is the MANAGEMENT (a caching mount, bidirectional sync, resumable
seeding), not the provider. One command,
.BR charon .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B charon sync " " [profile]
Reconcile the cache with its source, bidirectionally: all profiles, or one.
This is the primary operation, which is why the headline verb is
.B sync
rather than pull or push.
.TP
.B charon check " " [profile]
Audit and report drift, including whether each generated Unison profile still
matches what this version would write. Non-zero on drift.
.TP
.B charon status
Sources, profiles, and the artifacts they generate.
.TP
.B charon seed " " [--priority]
Bulk-populate the cache straight from the source, which is far faster than a
cold reconcile over a network filesystem.
.TP
.B charon install " " | " " uninstall
Provision the units, profiles and timers, or remove them.
.B uninstall
never touches the cache or your configuration.
.TP
.B charon source " " <list|show|probe|up|down> " " [name]
Manage the trees charon caches.
.B probe
MEASURES a backend's traits (case sensitivity, whether mtimes stick,
permissions, symlinks) and records them; the Unison profile is derived from
them. See TRAITS.
.PP
Every older spelling
.RB ( "charon sync run <p>" ", " "charon mount install" ", ...)"
still works, so a unit generated by an earlier version cannot break because a
command was renamed underneath it.
.SH LEGACY SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B charon mount " " <run|stop|status|install|uninstall>
Mount the configured rclone remote via FUSE at
.I ~/<remote>
with "feels-like-local" VFS-cache flags, and manage its systemd
.B --user
unit. Backend-specific flags (e.g. Google Drive's
.BR --drive-skip-gdocs )
are applied only to the matching remote type.
.TP
.B charon sync " " <run|seed|seed-full|install|config|check>
Keep the local cache
.RI ( ~/.<remote> )
in step with the mount using Unison; the mount is canonical. Sync is driven by
per-subtree PROFILES \(em nothing is baked in.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is data, in two kinds, both KEY=VALUE and read literally.
.SS Sources
A
.I ~/.config/charon/sources.d/<name>.conf
is one tree to cache and how it comes to exist:
.BR MOUNT " (the source root), " CACHE_ROOT " (the local cache root),"
.B PROVIDER
.RB ( rclone ", or " none " for a tree something else provides: an fstab entry,"
an automounted share, a local directory) and provider config such as
.BR REMOTE .
.PP
A source named
.B default
is IMPLICIT, derived from
.BR CHARON_REMOTE ,
so a single-remote install needs no sources.d file at all.
.SS Profiles
A
.I ~/.config/charon/profiles.d/<name>.conf
is one subtree to reconcile, plus its policy:
.B SOURCE
.RI ( <source>:<subtree> ", or the legacy " SUBTREE= " shorthand),"
.BR INTERVAL / BOOT / JITTER " (the timer cadence), " SEED_PRIORITY
(seeded synchronously at install) and
.B SEED_FULL_ORDER
(join the background full seed, ascending).
.PP
Several profiles normally share one source, which is why the two are separate:
the mount path and the remote name are one fact, and putting them on the
profile would let two profiles disagree about the same tree.
.PP
Any number of sources may be declared. Each rclone-backed source gets its own
mount unit,
.IR charon-mount@<source>.service ,
and each profile is ordered after the mount of the source it draws on, so one
machine can cache several remotes at once. A
.B PROVIDER=none
source gets no unit: something else owns that tree.
.PP
There is deliberately no working-link key. charon manages a cache; where a
desktop surfaces that cache is layout, and layout belongs to the integrator.
.SS Policy
Three knobs, in charon's own vocabulary rather than the reconciler's, so that
the underlying engine stays replaceable:
.TP
.B IGNORE
a path to exclude; repeatable. A value containing
.RB ' / '
is a path from the root of the subtree, anything else matches that name at any
depth.
.TP
.B CONFLICT
which side wins when both changed:
.BR remote " (default; the overwritten local copy is kept, locally), "
.BR local " (the overwritten copy then lands on the REMOTE), or " newer ,
which carries that same caveat whenever the cache is the newer side.
.TP
.B DELETE
.BR propagate " (default) or " never .
With
.B never
a deletion is not propagated, so the far copy survives, but the path becomes an
unresolved conflict and the profile FAILS every pass until a human settles it
by hand \(em restoring the file, or deleting it on both sides. It does NOT
silently restore the missing copy.
.PP
charon ships
.IR share/charon/example.conf " and " share/charon/example-source.conf ;
.B "setup.sh bootstrap"
copies the former into an empty profiles.d to get you started.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHARON_REMOTE
the rclone remote name (default
.BR gdrive );
the mount
.RI ( ~/$CHARON_REMOTE )
and cache
.RI ( ~/.$CHARON_REMOTE )
derive from it.
.TP
.B CHARON_MOUNT ", " CHARON_CACHE
override the derived mount / cache paths.
.TP
.B CHARON_RCLONE_PEER
an SSH host to copy the rclone remote stanza from (first-machine bootstrap).
.TP
.B CHARON_NOTIFY
an optional command
.RB ( "flag <id> <msg>" " / " "clear <id>" " / " list )
charon calls on sync faults; empty = no notify.
.SH TRAITS
Whether a backend folds case, carries mtimes, has Unix permissions or symlinks
is a fact about the backend, not a preference, and a wrong answer is expensive:
one such mismatch can make Unison refuse an entire profile. charon therefore
MEASURES them, with
.BR "charon source probe" ,
and derives the generated profile from the result.
.PP
Probing happens at CONFIG time only \(em at
.B install
when a source has none, or on the explicit verb \(em never during a sync pass,
because a probe writes. A failed probe records NOTHING: an uncertain
measurement is worse than none, because it becomes an assertion nobody
re-checks. Traits live under
.I ~/.local/state/charon/traits/
and are printed by
.BR "charon source show" .
.PP
Because the profile is derived from them, re-probing makes the live profile
stale and
.B charon check
says so: probe, check, install.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B charon sync
distinguishes a pass that did WORK from one that did NOTHING, because the two
are otherwise indistinguishable to a caller and to a log reader.
.TP
.B 0
the pass ran and succeeded.
.TP
.B 75
.RB ( EX_TEMPFAIL )
SKIPPED: no work was done, because the remote was offline or rate-limited, or
another pass held the lock. The next timer retries. The generated systemd unit
lists this under
.B SuccessExitStatus
so an offline machine does not accumulate failed units for an expected
condition, while
.B charon check
reads the unit's
.B ExecMainStatus
and reports it as SKIPPED rather than OK.
.TP
.B other
a fault. The reason is always logged, and additionally sent to
.B CHARON_NOTIFY
when one is configured.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/charon/sources.d/*.conf
the sources: what to cache, and how it comes to exist.
.TP
.I ~/.config/charon/profiles.d/*.conf
the profiles: what to reconcile, and with what policy.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/charon/traits/*
the MEASURED backend traits each profile is derived from.
.TP
.I ~/.unison/charon-<name>.prf
the generated Unison profiles.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/charon-mount@.service
the mount unit template, instanced once per rclone-backed source.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/charon-sync@<profile>.service.d/10-source.conf
generated ordering: binds a profile to its own source's mount unit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rclone (1),
.BR unison (1)
